Sort a copy numerically, then backtrack using nondecreasing candidate indices. Reusing the chosen index allows unlimited reuse; ascending traversal produces unique combinations in numeric lexicographic order. Target zero returns `[[]]`; an unreachable target returns `[]`.

```javascript
function solve(candidates, target) {
  const sorted = candidates.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const result = [];
  const path = [];

  function search(start, remaining) {
    if (remaining === 0) {
      result.push(path.slice());
      return;
    }

    for (let i = start; i < sorted.length; i++) {
      const value = sorted[i];
      if (value > remaining) break;

      path.push(value);
      search(i, remaining - value);
      path.pop();
    }
  }

  search(0, target);
  return result;
}
```

Call `solve(candidates, target)` directly with distinct positive candidates; no imports, exports, or I/O are needed.

Complexity: Let n be the candidate count, V the number of search calls, D the maximum combination length, and L the total number of integers returned across K combinations. Time is O(n log n + (n + 1)V + L), exponential in the worst case. Auxiliary space is O(n + D), plus O(K + L) for the returned result.

