Backtracking maintains 0 ≤ closed ≤ opened ≤ n. Exploring '(' before ')' produces lexicographic order; n=0 returns [""].

```javascript
function solve(n) {
  const result = [];
  const path = [];

  function search(opened, closed) {
    if (closed === n) {
      result.push(path.join(''));
      return;
    }

    if (opened < n) {
      path.push('(');
      search(opened + 1, closed);
      path.pop();
    }

    if (closed < opened) {
      path.push(')');
      search(opened, closed + 1);
      path.pop();
    }
  }

  search(0, 0);
  return result;
}
```

Call solve(n) directly with a nonnegative integer n; no imports, exports, or I/O are needed.

For n ≥ 1, time is O(nCₙ), where Cₙ = binomial(2n,n)/(n+1) is the number of results. Auxiliary space is O(n), excluding O(nCₙ) output storage. For n=0, time and space are O(1).

